TASK: Research-Driven Data Visualization Engine

Role: Senior Data Researcher and Lead Frontend Engineer. Research live data,
build a dashboard, then verify it runs clean in a real browser.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
HARD CONSTRAINTS

* You CANNOT see images. Never call take_screenshot. Use only
  list_console_messages and list_network_requests from the chrome-devtools
  MCP server.
* For any file over 200 lines, write it with a terminal heredoc
  (cat <<'EOF' > file), not the editor tool — the editor call will fail on
  large inputs.
* Never report the result of a check you did not run. If you did not run a
  tool, say so explicitly rather than describing what it would have returned.
* Do not tell me the task is complete until PHASE 4 passes.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PHASE 1 — RESEARCH

Objective: the top 10 open-weight coding LLMs as of today.

1. Live search required. Your training data is older than today and will
   name models that have been superseded.
2. Establish today's actual date by tool call. Do not assume it, and do not
   use a date you cannot source.
3. Use LiveCodeBench (Generation) as the primary axis. Fetch the published
   results directly from:
      https://livecodebench.github.io/performances_generation.json
   Do not use the interactive leaderboard — it requires authentication. The
   JSON does not. That file is PER-QUESTION results, not a leaderboard: each
   entry is one question's pass@1. Compute each model's score by grouping on
   model and averaging. Write a script; do not estimate.
4. Do NOT use a composite, aggregate, or "index" score. A number averaged
   across many benchmarks is a mix of scoring scales even when presented as a
   single figure, and a general-intelligence composite does not measure
   coding.
5. Source each score from the benchmark's own published results or the model
   card. Do not cite aggregator sites, blog roundups, or SEO listicles. Cite
   a source URL for every single score.
6. Open weights only. The weights must be publicly downloadable. Exclude
   API-only tiers (e.g. "Max" or "Plus" tiers) and anything you cannot
   confirm is downloadable.
7. If a model has no published score on the chosen benchmark, exclude it.
   NEVER invent, estimate, normalise, hand-curate, or use "(est.)". Fewer
   real rows beats more fabricated ones. If you cannot fetch the data, stop
   and say so — do not substitute values from memory.
8. For each model record: total parameters, active parameters if MoE,
   approximate VRAM at 4-bit, and whether it fits in 16GB.

VRAM RULE — weights are not the whole cost.
   * Dense models: add KV cache and compute buffer, and reserve ~1GB. A model
     whose 4-bit weights alone reach 16GB does NOT fit. Treat ~13GB of
     weights as the practical ceiling.
   * MoE models: experts can be offloaded to system RAM, so total size
     matters far less than active parameters. Judge against available system
     RAM rather than VRAM.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PHASE 2 — DEPENDENCIES

Objective: ensure the file actually runs in a vanilla browser.

1. Use React and MUI (Material UI), UMD builds from unpkg.
2. Before writing any <script src>, FETCH each URL and confirm it returns
   HTTP 200. Do not assume a status you have not observed. If you cannot
   fetch a URL, say so explicitly rather than guessing.
3. Do not guess filenames or casing, and do not assume a library ships a
   .min build. If unsure of a file's exact name, fetch the package listing
   and read it.
4. Identify and load peer dependencies as globals, in the correct order.
   MUI v5 requires @emotion/react and @emotion/styled before it loads.
5. Use Babel standalone for JSX. No build step, no bundler.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PHASE 3 — BUILD

Output as separate files, each under 200 lines:
   index.html      shell and script tags only
   data.js         the dataset
   components.jsx  chart components
   app.jsx         dashboard layout and render

Load the .jsx files with <script type="text/babel" src="...">.

AFTER WRITING, run `ls -la` in the project directory and confirm that every
<script src> which is NOT a URL corresponds to a file that actually exists.
A referenced-but-unwritten local file 404s at load time and leaves the page
blank.

Content:
* Horizontal bar chart of the benchmark score, 0-100, sorted best first.
* Two donut charts: "Parameter Scale Distribution" and "Hardware
  Accessibility (fits 16GB VRAM)". These must use DIFFERENT datasets.
* A data table: model, score, benchmark, params, active params, VRAM,
  16GB fit, source link.
* MUI components: Paper, Table, LinearProgress, Chip, Tooltip.
* Title, plus a caption naming the exact benchmark and the date retrieved.
* Tooltips showing score, benchmark, parameters and VRAM.

If any SVG arc mathematics is uncertain, use MUI components instead of
hand-written SVG paths — a malformed SVG renders blank with no error.

════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PHASE 4 — VERIFY (do not skip)

1. Serve the directory: python3 -m http.server 8000
   External JSX will NOT load from file:// — Babel fetches it by XHR and the
   browser blocks that as cross-origin.
2. Using the chrome-devtools MCP server, navigate to http://localhost:8000
   and call list_console_messages.
3. Report every message verbatim with its source.
4. Fix every error, then reload and re-check. Repeat until clean.
5. Also call list_network_requests and confirm no request returned a non-200
   — a 404'd script produces a blank page with no thrown error, and this is
   the only way to see a missing local file.
6. Assert the chart encodes its data. Read the computed width of each bar's
   fill element and confirm it is proportional to its value. A bar with a
   wrong width renders without error and the console cannot detect it.

"Clean" means zero errors and zero warnings EXCEPT this one, which is
expected and must NOT be chased:
   [warn] You are using the in-browser Babel transformer...

